.TH "FLOX\-EDIT" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-edit \- edit the declarative environment configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general options>] edit
     [\-d=<path> | \-r=<owner/name>]
     [[\-f=<file>] | \-n=<name> | \-\-sync | \-\-reset]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Transactionally edit the environment manifest.
By default, invokes an editor with a copy of the local manifest for the
user to interactively edit.
The editor is found by querying \f[CR]$EDITOR\f[R], \f[CR]$VISUAL\f[R],
and then by looking for common editors in \f[CR]$PATH\f[R].
The manifest of an environment on FloxHub or in a different directory
can be edited via the \f[CR]\-r\f[R] or \f[CR]\-d\f[R] flags
respectively.
See \f[B]\f[CB]manifest.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for more details on the
manifest format.
.PP
Once the editor is closed the environment is built in order to validate
the edit.
If the build fails you are given a chance to continue editing the
manifest, and if you decline, the edit is discarded.
This transactional editing prevents an edit from leaving the environment
in a broken state.
One exception is the \f[CR]\-n\f[R] flag, which renames a local
environment but does not rebuild it.
.PP
The environment can be edited non\-interactively via the \f[CR]\-f\f[R]
flag, which replaces the contents of the manifest with those of the
provided file.
.SS Sync the local manifest with the current generation.
When using environments that were pushed to or pulled from FloxHub,
changes to the local manifest in \f[CR].flox/env/manifest.toml\f[R] will
block the use of the environment commands
\f[CR]flox {install, uninstall, edit, upgrade}\f[R].
To proceed, you can run either:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]flox edit \-\-sync\f[R] to commit your local changes to a new
generation
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]flox edit \-\-reset\f[R] to discard your local changes and reset
to the latest generation
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Edit Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-file\f[R]
Replace environment manifest with that in \f[CR]<file>\f[R].
If \f[CR]<file>\f[R] is \f[CR]\-\f[R], reads from stdin.
.TP
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-name\f[R]
Rename the environment to \f[CR]<name>\f[R].
Only works for local environments.
.TP
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-sync\f[R]
Create a new generation from the current local environment (Only
available for managed environments)
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-reset\f[R]
Reset the environment to the current generation (Only available for
managed environments)
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.TP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-reference\f[R]
A FloxHub environment, specified in the form \f[CR]<owner>/<name>\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-default\f[R]
Use your default environment (\f[CR]<your\-user>/default\f[R]).
When unauthenticated in an interactive context, you will be prompted to
log in.
In non\-interactive contexts (e.g., scripts or CI), this flag will fail
with an error when authentication is missing.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\f[CR]$EDITOR\f[R], \f[CR]$VISUAL\f[R]
Override the default editor used for editing environment manifests and
commit messages.
